feat(ai-triage): post form submissions to the triage backend

The toolkit's triage form has only ever sent its answers as two Notify
emails, while the backend's POST /submissions has sat there with no
caller and a queue that fills only when someone types into it by hand.
This joins the two halves of intake.

The post is an addition, not a replacement. Both emails still go, and
the post happens after them, so a submission can never reach the queue
without the mailbox email that carries the submitter's address. Any
failure is logged with the reference and swallowed: a backend problem
must not cost someone their submission or change what they see.

The reference already on both emails is reused as the submissionId, so
one identifier names all three records and the backend's idempotency on
that key handles retries. The composed text carries the five
substantive answers and omits the email address, which the backend has
no field for and which would otherwise reach a hosted model.

Posts are signed with a short-lived AWS WebIdentity token, the calling
half of the service-to-service auth plan. Both the backend post and the
token default to off, so nothing changes until each is enabled per
environment.

The STS client is given explicit timeouts, because the SDK has none. The
token call sits in the submitter's path, ahead of the post and outside the
AbortSignal that bounds it, and it reaches STS directly rather than through
the forward proxy -- a path proven in Dev and nowhere else. @smithy's
setConnectionTimeout takes 0 as its default and returns immediately when it
is falsy, so an unbounded client somewhere that path is blocked would hold
the submit page open for the operating system's TCP timeout, once per
attempt. Both emails have already been sent by the time a token is asked
for, so failing quickly costs nothing and waiting costs the submitter.
Refs: CAIT-261
Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
